@prefix prim: <urn:silmaril:prim:#> .
@prefix silm: <urn:silmaril:entity#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# =====================================================================================
# formal.ttl — the FORMAL olog of the Primitive Floor (W2 · SP1, plan Task 2).
# =====================================================================================
# The yin facet of the twin-olog colimit taiji: the type-towers as a category whose
# objects are mathematical/logical types and whose rdfs:subClassOf arrows encode the
# ISA of the mathematics (a Natural IS an Integer IS a Rational ...). Nothing here
# bottoms out at a bare xsd: leaf; after the per-type build every one of the 59 tower
# classes is dual-grounded DIRECTLY — abstract supertypes and named sub-objects no less
# than concrete leaves each carry their OWN prim:Realization (prim:realizesFrom the class
# itself), their OWN reflexive physical carrier (an encoding whose prim:interpretAs is the
# class itself) and their OWN prim:Primitive — into the physical olog (physical.ttl /
# realization.ttl), and the colimit is sealed in taiji.ttl. The rdfs:subClassOf arrows are
# pure taxonomic subtyping, NOT the grounding mechanism. This grounding is not asserted but
# ENFORCED over the class form by the SHACL prim:DualGroundingShape, whose rdfs:subClassOf*
# walk still accepts self/ancestor/descendant so the class-form orphan probe bites — but no
# class relies on that fallback. Corpus-agnostic: pure shape, zero instances.
#
# Design spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-07-w2-primitive-floor-design.md (§3)
# Plan:        docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-07-w2-sp1-primitive-floor.md (Task 2)
# Acceptance:  q_number_tower (litmus half #1) turns GREEN with this file.
# =====================================================================================

prim:FormalType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The formal facet of the primitive floor: a mathematical or logical type considered independently of any machine encoding. FormalTypes are organised into towers by rdfs:subClassOf, which encodes the ISA of the mathematics — a Natural IS an Integer IS a Rational and so on. Dual-grounding is universal over the tower and, after the per-type build, per-node DIRECT: every one of the 59 tower classes — the abstract supertypes and named sub-objects (NaturalWithZero, URI/IRI/QName, Latitude/Longitude, Ordinal/Categorical) exactly like the concrete leaves (String, DateTime, Rational, Complex, Hash, …) — carries its OWN prim:Realization (prim:realizesFrom the class itself), its OWN reflexive carrier (an encoding whose prim:interpretAs is the class itself) and its OWN prim:Primitive, so each is grounded on ITSELF; the rdfs:subClassOf arrows stay pure taxonomic subtyping, not the grounding mechanism (spec §9a). This is not a prose promise but an ENFORCED law: the SHACL prim:DualGroundingShape walks rdfs:subClassOf* over the CLASS form and rejects any tower class with no realization on itself, an ancestor, or a descendant — that disjunctive tooth is retained so the injected owl:Class orphan is caught (conforms=False), though no class in this floor relies on the ancestor/descendant fallback. This is the yin of the colimit taiji whose yang is prim:PhysicalCarrier." .

# ---- Tower 1: Number (the litmus tower) ---------------------------------------------

prim:Complex a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Complex" ;
    rdfs:comment "The complex numbers ℂ — the top of the number tower for this iteration. Every Real is a Complex with zero imaginary part and every Imaginary is a Complex with zero real part, so Complex is the join of the two sub-facets. Physically realized as a pair of Real encodings (real, imaginary) or an interleaved 2×float byte-vector; the realization Frame carries the same IEEE-754 rounding effect as its Real components. Algebraic-vs-transcendental sub-facets are deferred (spec §8)." .

prim:Real a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Complex ;
    rdfs:label "Real" ;
    rdfs:comment "The real numbers ℝ. Physically realized through the IEEE-754 family — Float32 (4-byte) or Float64 (8-byte), each little- or big-endian — with the realization Frame carrying an IEEE754-rounding effect and, for a Float32 narrowed from a Float64 source, an additional narrowing-loss effect. Real is a subclass of Complex and a superclass of Rational; the what-is-a-number litmus asserts exactly this chain plus a correct-width realization at 64 and 32 bits." .

prim:Rational a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Real ;
    rdfs:label "Rational" ;
    rdfs:comment "The rational numbers ℚ — ratios p/q of an Integer numerator and a non-zero Integer denominator. Rational sits between Integer (its subtype, every integer is n/1) and Real (its supertype, every rational is a real). Its canonical physical realization is a numerator·denominator pair of integer encodings rather than a lossy float; converting a Rational to a fixed-width float carries a rounding effect and is a different, lossy realization. A distinct sibling Decimal handles base-10 fixed-point." .

prim:Integer a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Rational ;
    rdfs:label "Integer" ;
    rdfs:comment "The integers ℤ — the whole numbers with sign, closed under addition, subtraction and multiplication. Integer is a subclass of Rational (every n is n/1) and a superclass of Natural. Physically realized as the two's-complement INT8/INT16/INT32/INT64 machine widths (little- or big-endian) whose realization Frame carries a width-bounded overflow-domain effect; the arbitrary-precision escape hatch is the sibling Bignum, which trades the fixed width for a may-overflow-into-bytevector effect." .

prim:Natural a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Integer ;
    rdfs:label "Natural" ;
    rdfs:comment "The natural numbers ℕ — the non-negative (or strictly positive) whole numbers, the counting floor of the number tower. Natural is a subclass of Integer. Whether zero is included is genuinely convention-dependent, so the two readings are split into the named sub-objects NaturalWithZero and NaturalWithoutZero rather than force-fitting one. Physically realized as the unsigned UINT8/16/32/64 widths whose realization Frame carries a width-bounded overflow-domain effect." .

prim:NaturalWithZero a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Natural ;
    rdfs:label "NaturalWithZero" ;
    rdfs:comment "The natural numbers including zero, {0, 1, 2, ...} — the ℕ₀ reading used by set theory, computer science, and the recursive positive-count authority whose Unit denotes one and whose Successor(previous) denotes the next count. This is one of the two named sub-objects that resolve the honest ambiguity of whether zero is a natural number; it is realized by the unsigned integer widths with ordinal floor 0. The complementary reading is NaturalWithoutZero." .

prim:NaturalWithoutZero a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Natural ;
    rdfs:label "NaturalWithoutZero" ;
    rdfs:comment "The strictly positive natural numbers {1, 2, 3, ...} — the ℕ⁺ reading favoured by classical number theory where counting begins at one. This is the second of the two named sub-objects splitting the zero-inclusion ambiguity rather than silently picking a side (Präriehund honesty: name the fork, do not force-fit). It is realized by the unsigned integer widths interpreted with an ordinal floor of 1 rather than 0." .

prim:Imaginary a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Complex ;
    rdfs:label "Imaginary" ;
    rdfs:comment "The imaginary numbers — real multiples of the imaginary unit i where i² = −1, a facet of Complex complementary to Real. Every Imaginary is a Complex with zero real part, so it is a direct subclass of Complex and NOT of Real; the litmus tower asserts prim:Imaginary rdfs:subClassOf prim:Complex precisely to witness that the top of the number tower is the join of a real axis and an imaginary axis. Realized as a single Real encoding tagged as the imaginary coordinate." .

prim:Bignum a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Integer ;
    rdfs:label "Bignum" ;
    rdfs:comment "An arbitrary-precision integer — the unbounded escape hatch from the fixed machine widths, a sibling of Decimal within the number tower and a subclass of Integer in value (it denotes an integer) while differing in realization (no fixed width). Its canonical realization is a prim:ByteVector of 1..128 byte occurrences, so the unbounded mathematical value meets a bounded physical carrier; that mismatch is exactly the typed may-overflow effect its realization Frame carries." .

prim:Decimal a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Rational ;
    rdfs:label "Decimal" ;
    rdfs:comment "A base-10 fixed-point number — the exact-decimal sibling of Bignum within the number tower and a subclass of Rational, since every terminating decimal is a rational with a power-of-ten denominator. Decimal exists precisely because the binary IEEE-754 realizations of Real cannot represent values such as 0.1 exactly; its realization keeps a base-10 coefficient and scale, so its Frame carries a base-10-precision effect rather than binary IEEE754-rounding." .

# ---- Tower 2: Boolean ---------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Kleene3 a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Kleene3" ;
    rdfs:comment "Three-valued Kleene logic — the truth values true, false, and unknown (the third, indeterminate value). Kleene3 is the supertype of Bool because the two-valued Boolean algebra embeds into it by simply never using the unknown value. It is the formal home of the SQL NULL and of the structured-versus-unstructured indeterminacy the corpus work needs downstream; physically realized in a small integer or a two-bit field rather than a single bit, since three states do not fit one bit." .

prim:Bool a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Kleene3 ;
    rdfs:label "Bool" ;
    rdfs:comment "The classical two-valued Boolean type — true or false, the truth-value floor of propositional logic. Bool is modelled as a subclass of Kleene3 (it is exactly Kleene three-valued logic with the unknown value forbidden), which gives the Boolean tower a genuine subtyping arrow rather than a flat pair. Physically realized as a single Bit (width 1) or, following the UEFI BOOLEAN and C conventions, as a UINT8 holding 0 or 1; the realization carries no lossy effect." .

# ---- Tower 3: Text ------------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Codepoint a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Codepoint" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Unicode scalar value — a single code point in the range U+0000..U+10FFFF excluding the surrogate range, the atom of text in this tower. A Codepoint is an abstract number identifying a character; it is not yet bytes. It is realized by the text encodings UTF-8 (1..4 bytes), UTF-16 (2 or 4 bytes, LE or BE) and UTF-32 (4 bytes) whose realization Frames carry an encoding-loss effect covering surrogate handling and normalization. Codepoints compose into Graphemes and Strings." .

prim:Grapheme a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Grapheme" ;
    rdfs:comment "A grapheme cluster — a user-perceived character, which may be a single Codepoint or a base codepoint plus combining marks (an accented letter, an emoji with modifiers, a regional-indicator flag pair). Grapheme is a direct subclass of FormalType and deliberately NOT a subclass of Codepoint, because a cluster is a sequence of codepoints, not a kind of codepoint; asserting a false subtyping arrow would violate the honesty rule. It is the human-visible unit of text between Codepoint and String." .

prim:String a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "String" ;
    rdfs:comment "A string — a finite ordered sequence of Codepoints, the composite top of the text tower. String is a direct subclass of FormalType rather than of Codepoint or Grapheme because it is built from them by sequence composition, not by subtyping. Physically a String is realized by choosing a text encoding for its codepoints and laying the resulting byte occurrences into a prim:ByteVector or Stream; the encoding choice and its loss profile are recorded on the realization Frame, not on the abstract String." .

# ---- Tower 4: Temporal --------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Instant a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Instant" ;
    rdfs:comment "A point on the time line — an absolute instant independent of any calendar or timezone, the temporal analogue of a real-number point. An Instant is what a physical clock reads; it is realized either as a signed INT64 count of a fixed epoch tick (seconds or nanoseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) with an epoch-precision effect, or as an ISO-8601 lexical string with a lexical effect. Duration measures the gap between two Instants; DateTime is a civil naming of one." .

prim:Duration a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Duration" ;
    rdfs:comment "A length of time — a signed or unsigned quantity of temporal displacement with no anchor on the time line, the difference of two Instants. Duration is distinct from Interval (which is anchored, a span between two named endpoints) and from Instant (a point). It is realized as an integer count of a fixed tick unit or as an ISO-8601 duration lexical form; conversions between tick granularities carry a precision effect. It composes with Instant by translation: Instant + Duration = Instant." .

prim:Interval a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Interval" ;
    rdfs:comment "An anchored span of time — a pair of endpoints on the time line (a start Instant and either an end Instant or a Duration), unlike the unanchored Duration. Interval is the temporal home of validity ranges, event windows, and the half-open [start, end) conventions that avoid double-counting boundaries. It is realized as a product of two Instant encodings or an Instant-plus-Duration encoding; the endpoint inclusivity is a facet recorded on the realization rather than assumed." .

prim:Date a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Date" ;
    rdfs:comment "A calendar date — a year-month-day triple in a stated calendar (Gregorian by default), naming a whole day rather than an instant. Date is a civil, timezone-free coordinate: the same Date denotes different Instants in different zones, so it is a direct subclass of FormalType and not of Instant. It is realized as a packed integer (for example days since an epoch, or a zero-padded ISO-8601 YYYY-MM-DD lexical string); calendar-reform and proleptic edge cases are facets of the realization." .

prim:Time a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Time" ;
    rdfs:comment "A time of day — an hour-minute-second(-fraction) coordinate within an unspecified day, the daily-cycle complement of Date. Time alone does not name an Instant; it must be paired with a Date and a timezone to do so, which is why it is a direct subclass of FormalType. It is realized as an integer count of sub-day ticks (seconds or nanoseconds since midnight) or an ISO-8601 hh:mm:ss lexical string; leap-second handling is a documented facet of the realization." .

prim:DateTime a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "DateTime" ;
    rdfs:comment "A civil date-and-time — a Date combined with a Time and, via a timezone facet, optionally an offset that pins it to an absolute Instant. DateTime is the human-facing naming of a moment; when the timezone facet is present it is convertible to and from an Instant, and when absent it is a floating local coordinate. It is realized as a composite integer record or an ISO-8601 lexical string with offset; the presence or absence of the offset is the load-bearing timezone facet." .

# ---- Tower 5: Identifier (URN-primacy per the unary law) ----------------------------

prim:URN a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "URN" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Uniform Resource Name (Resource/Uniform/Name in the full-lexical unary-law spelling) — the load-bearing root of the identifier tower and of the whole ontology, since S/O/P are URN towers and every coordinate is URN-addressed. This floor deliberately treats URN as the most general identifier abstraction: URI and IRI are modelled as its subtypes, inverting the W3C containment convention on purpose to honour the unary law URN-primacy. Realized as an interpreted String over a byte carrier." .

prim:URI a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:URN ;
    rdfs:label "URI" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Uniform Resource Identifier (Resource/Uniform/Identifier) — an ASCII-restricted global identifier. Modelled here as a subclass of URN following the spec's explicit URN ⊃ URI ⊃ IRI ordering; note this deliberately inverts the classical W3C reading in which a URN is a kind of URI, a documented design inversion chosen so the URN root stays load-bearing rather than an oversight. A URI is realized as an interpreted ASCII String laid into a byte carrier; percent-encoding of non-ASCII octets is a realization facet." .

prim:IRI a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:URI ;
    rdfs:label "IRI" ;
    rdfs:comment "An Internationalized Resource Identifier — a URI generalised to the full Unicode repertoire rather than ASCII only. Modelled as a subclass of URI within the spec's URN ⊃ URI ⊃ IRI chain; every IRI maps to a URI by percent-encoding its non-ASCII codepoints, so the subtyping arrow tracks that lossless embedding direction. It is realized as an interpreted UTF-8 String over a byte carrier, and its relationship to the ASCII URI form is a normalization facet of the realization." .

prim:QName a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:IRI ;
    rdfs:label "QName" ;
    rdfs:comment "A qualified name or compact IRI (the CURIE form) — a prefix and local part such as prim:Real that expands to a full IRI against an in-scope prefix map. QName is modelled as a subclass of IRI because it denotes an IRI in an abbreviated lexical form; it is a representation shortcut, not a distinct kind of resource, and its expansion depends on the surrounding namespace bindings. Realized as an interpreted String plus the prefix-resolution context needed to expand it." .

prim:BlankNode a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "BlankNode" ;
    rdfs:comment "A blank node — a local existential identifier with no global name, denoting there-exists-something without saying what. BlankNode is a direct subclass of FormalType and deliberately NOT of URN, because a blank node is precisely the identifier that has no Uniform Resource Name; placing it under URN would be a force-fit the honesty rule forbids. Its scope is a single graph or document, and it is realized as a graph-local label (for example _:b0) that must be freshened on merge." .

# ---- Tower 6: RDF-term --------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Literal a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Literal" ;
    rdfs:comment "An RDF literal — a lexical form plus either a datatype IRI (a typed literal) or a language tag (a language-tagged string). Literal is the bridge from the RDF-term tower back into the value towers: its datatype grounds it into Number, Text, or Temporal via the W3C datatype algebra, so a typed literal is dual-natured (an RDF term on one side, a realized value on the other). Realized as an interpreted String for the lexical form together with the datatype or language coordinate." .

prim:Triple a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Triple" ;
    rdfs:comment "An RDF triple — an ordered subject-predicate-object statement, the atomic proposition of the graph. In the unary law S, O and P are independently typed URN towers, so a Triple is a product of three identifier/term coordinates rather than a flat row. Triple is a direct subclass of FormalType; a Quad extends it with a graph-name coordinate but is not a subtype of it. Realized as a product of its three navigable term encodings, each descending to the byte carrier." .

prim:Quad a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Quad" ;
    rdfs:comment "An RDF quad — a Triple extended with a fourth coordinate naming the graph (or context) the statement belongs to, the unit of a dataset of named graphs. Quad is a direct subclass of FormalType and not of Triple: adding a coordinate makes a wider product, not a narrower subtype, so asserting Quad ⊂ Triple would be a false arrow. Realized as a product of four navigable term encodings; the graph coordinate is itself an Identifier-tower value." .

prim:RDFGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "RDFGraph" ;
    rdfs:comment "An RDF graph — a set of Triples, the composite top of the RDF-term tower and the shape the whole ontology is expressed in. RDFGraph is a direct subclass of FormalType built by set-composition over Triple, mirroring how the Aggregate-tower Set is built over its element type. It is realized by serializing its triples into a byte stream through a concrete syntax (Turtle, N-Triples, and so on); the choice of syntax and its round-trip fidelity are facets of that realization." .

# ---- Tower 7: Aggregate -------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Tuple a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Tuple" ;
    rdfs:comment "A tuple or finite product — an ordered, fixed-length, heterogeneous collection whose components may each have a different type, the categorical product of the Aggregate tower. Tuple is the shape behind a Rational numerator·denominator pair, a Complex real·imaginary pair, and the Frame output·effect product. It is realized by concatenating the byte encodings of its components in order, with alignment and padding facets recorded on the realization; its arity is fixed and part of its identity." .

prim:List a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "List" ;
    rdfs:comment "A list or sequence — an ordered, variable-length, homogeneous collection of elements of one element type, distinct from the fixed heterogeneous Tuple. List is the ordered-sequence workhorse of the Aggregate tower and the parent of the fixed-length Vector. It is realized as a length-prefixed or terminator-delimited run of element encodings laid into a byte stream; order is part of its identity, so unlike Set and Bag it never reorders, and duplicate elements are significant and positional." .

prim:Vector a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:List ;
    rdfs:label "Vector" ;
    rdfs:comment "A vector — a fixed-length homogeneous ordered sequence of numeric components, modelled as a subclass of List (a Vector is a List whose length is pinned and whose elements are numbers). Vector is the rank-1 case of Tensor and feeds the AOB tensor block and the Lambda vector families above the byte carrier. It is realized as a dense contiguous run of equal-width element encodings with a stated component order and endianness, so its byte layout is directly addressable by index." .

prim:Tensor a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Tensor" ;
    rdfs:comment "A tensor — an n-dimensional array of numeric components with a shape (a tuple of per-axis extents), generalising the rank-1 Vector to arbitrary rank. Tensor is a direct subclass of FormalType because it carries shape and stride structure a plain List does not; a rank-1 Tensor is isomorphic to a Vector but the two are kept distinct so the shape metadata is explicit. It is realized as a dense byte block plus a shape-and-stride descriptor, feeding the AOB tensor block." .

prim:Bag a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Bag" ;
    rdfs:comment "A bag or multiset — an unordered collection in which elements may repeat and the multiplicity of each element is significant, unlike a Set (multiplicities capped at one) and unlike a List (order significant). Bag is the parent of Set in this tower: a Set is exactly a Bag whose every multiplicity is at most one. It is realized as a run of element encodings with no positional meaning together with the per-element counts; any serialization order is an arbitrary realization facet, not identity." .

prim:Set a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Bag ;
    rdfs:label "Set" ;
    rdfs:comment "A set — an unordered collection of distinct elements, where neither order nor repetition is significant. Set is modelled as a subclass of Bag (a Set is a Bag whose element multiplicities are all at most one), giving the Aggregate tower a genuine subtyping arrow instead of a flat family. It is the shape behind an RDFGraph (a set of Triples). Realized as a run of distinct element encodings with a canonical ordering chosen only for byte-level determinism, not for identity." .

prim:Map a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Map" ;
    rdfs:comment "A map, dictionary, or associative array — a finite set of key-to-value entries with unique keys, a partial function from a key type to a value type. Map is a direct subclass of FormalType; it can be seen as a Set of key·value Tuples with a key-uniqueness constraint, but it is kept as its own object so the functional-lookup semantics are explicit. It is realized as a serialized run of key-value encoding pairs, with any hashing or ordering being a realization facet rather than part of its identity." .

# ---- Tower 8: Algebraic constructors (mirror the unary law) -------------------------

prim:Sum a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Sum" ;
    rdfs:comment "A sum type, coproduct, or tagged union — a value that is exactly one of several typed alternatives, carrying a discriminating tag that says which. Sum is the categorical dual of the Tuple product and the parent of Optional. It is the shape behind the byte-vector Empty|Link alternatives and the admission Accepted|Rejected state. Realized as a tag encoding (selecting the active alternative) followed by the encoding of that alternative's payload; the tag width is a realization facet." .

prim:Optional a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Sum ;
    rdfs:label "Optional" ;
    rdfs:comment "An optional or maybe type — a value that is either present (some payload) or absent (none), modelled as a subclass of Sum because it is exactly the two-alternative tagged union none | some(value). Optional is how this floor represents nullability without conflating it with Kleene3 unknown: absence of a value is a structural fact, not a third truth value. Realized as a one-bit-or-one-tag presence flag followed, when present, by the payload encoding." .

prim:Frame a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Frame" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Frame — the unary law's Yoneda point: the typed product of an output coordinate and exactly one error-or-effect coordinate that a callable returns around a byte stream. Frame is the formal type that the realization monad ρ is the Kleisli category of; realizing any FormalType yields a Frame(output: encoding, effect: rounding/loss/endianness). It is a product like Tuple but with the two coordinates fixed as output and effect. Its realization is the physical Frame carrier of the byte-frame law." .

prim:Unit a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Unit" ;
    rdfs:comment "The unit type — the terminal object of the formal category, a type with exactly one value carrying no information (the empty product, the nullary Tuple). Unit is the identity for the product and the codomain every value maps to trivially; it is the monadic unit's natural home. NOTE: this is the type-theoretic terminal Unit, deliberately kept distinct from the measurement UnitOfMeasure of the Quantity tower to avoid a name collision. Realized as a zero-width encoding." .

prim:Void a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Void" ;
    rdfs:comment "The void or empty type — the initial object of the formal category, a type with no values at all (the empty coproduct, the nullary Sum). Void is the dual of Unit: where Unit has exactly one value, Void has none, so a value of Void can never be constructed and a function from Void exists uniquely and vacuously. It marks the unreachable and the impossible in the type algebra. It IS dual-grounded — realized by the empty 0-byte encoding prim:VoidEmpty (zero bits, zero bytes, zero occurrences) whose realization Frame carries the prim:Uninhabited effect — so universality stays total over the tower without a dodge (spec §9f). That realization is honest precisely because it is uninhabited: it is the physical nothing of the empty coproduct, NOT an inhabited encoding, and any encoding claiming an actual Void value would be a contradiction. This is the one genuine edge where the typed effect, rather than a value, carries the meaning." .

# ---- Tower 9: Quantity --------------------------------------------------------------

prim:Quantity a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Quantity" ;
    rdfs:comment "A quantity — a magnitude paired with a unit of measure, such as 5 kilograms or 300 kelvin, the composite root of the Quantity tower. Quantity is a product of a Number-tower magnitude and a UnitOfMeasure; it is a direct subclass of FormalType and the parent of Measurement (a Quantity plus an uncertainty). It is realized by realizing its magnitude as a numeric encoding and tagging it with a unit identifier; unit conversions are typed arrows carrying a rescaling effect." .

prim:Dimension a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Dimension" ;
    rdfs:comment "A physical dimension — the kind of a quantity independent of any particular unit, such as length, mass, time, or a product like length·time⁻¹ for velocity. Dimension classifies which units are mutually convertible: two quantities can be compared or added only when their Dimensions match. It is a direct subclass of FormalType. Its CURRENT physical realization is a SCALAR UINT8 registry index (prim:DimensionUint8 / prim:DimensionExponents, one octet, cardinality 256) that names one entry in an enumerated registry of base dimensions and their products; the encoding is exactly that single integer label, so its only lossless operation is registry lookup, not arithmetic on components. The full dimensional-formula reading — a VECTOR of integer exponents over the base dimensions, with dimensional analysis reduced to integer vector arithmetic — is a CONCEPTUAL/DEFERRED model recovered by looking the index up in the registry; it is NOT the current encoding, which stores no exponent components to compute over." .

prim:UnitOfMeasure a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "UnitOfMeasure" ;
    rdfs:comment "A unit of measure — a named reference magnitude for a given Dimension, such as metre, second, kilogram, or a derived unit like newton. NOTE: named UnitOfMeasure rather than Unit precisely to avoid colliding with the type-theoretic terminal prim:Unit of the Algebraic tower (Präriehund honesty: name the collision, do not overload). It is the parent of Currency and is realized as a unit identifier plus a conversion factor and offset to the SI base of its Dimension." .

prim:Currency a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    rdfs:label "Currency" ;
    rdfs:comment "A currency — a monetary unit of measure such as an ISO 4217 code (USD, EUR, JPY), modelled as a subclass of UnitOfMeasure because a currency is the reference magnitude for the dimension of monetary value. Unlike physical units its conversion factors are time-varying exchange rates rather than fixed constants, which is a documented facet of any currency-conversion realization. A monetary Quantity is a magnitude tagged with a Currency, realized as a Decimal amount plus the currency code." .

prim:Measurement a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Quantity ;
    rdfs:label "Measurement" ;
    rdfs:comment "A measurement — a Quantity enriched with an uncertainty (an error bar, standard deviation, or confidence interval), modelled as a subclass of Quantity because every measurement is a quantity that additionally carries how well it is known. Measurement is where empirical values live: a bare Quantity asserts an exact magnitude, a Measurement admits its own imprecision. Realized as the value encoding plus a paired uncertainty encoding in the same unit, so propagation of error is explicit." .

# ---- Tower 10: Geospatial -----------------------------------------------------------

prim:Coordinate a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Coordinate" ;
    rdfs:comment "A coordinate — a single scalar position along one axis of a coordinate reference system, the atom of the geospatial tower and the parent of Latitude and Longitude. A Coordinate has meaning only relative to its CRS, so it always travels with one. It is a direct subclass of FormalType, realized as a Real-tower encoding (a float or a fixed-point angle in degrees) whose interpretation as an axis position is fixed by the associated CRS and its axis order." .

prim:Point a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Point" ;
    rdfs:comment "A point geometry — a single location given by a tuple of Coordinates (for example longitude and latitude, optionally with elevation) in a stated CRS, the zero-dimensional geometry of the WKT/GeoJSON family. Point is a direct subclass of FormalType and the building block of Line and Polygon. It is realized as an ordered tuple of coordinate encodings plus a CRS reference; the axis order (lon-lat versus lat-lon) is a load-bearing facet recorded on the realization, not guessed." .

prim:Line a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Line" ;
    rdfs:comment "A line or linestring geometry — an ordered sequence of two or more Points describing a one-dimensional path (a polyline), the one-dimensional geometry of the WKT/GeoJSON family. Line is a direct subclass of FormalType built by sequence-composition over Point; its identity includes vertex order and count. It is realized as a length-prefixed run of point encodings sharing one CRS, with the same axis-order facet as its constituent points recorded once for the whole geometry." .

prim:Polygon a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Polygon" ;
    rdfs:comment "A polygon geometry — a two-dimensional area bounded by one closed exterior ring of Points and zero or more interior rings (holes), the two-dimensional geometry of the WKT/GeoJSON family. Polygon is a direct subclass of FormalType; ring closure (first point equals last) and winding order are part of its well-formedness. It is realized as a run of closed coordinate rings sharing one CRS, with winding-order and ring-role facets recorded so exterior and holes are unambiguous." .

prim:CRS a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "CRS" ;
    rdfs:comment "A coordinate reference system — the frame that gives Coordinates and geometries their meaning, pairing a datum (an origin and shape of the earth model) with a coordinate system and axis order, identified for example by an EPSG code such as EPSG:4326. CRS is a direct subclass of FormalType and the context every Point, Line, and Polygon must reference. It seeds the S/O/P-tower CRS sub-project; it is realized as a CRS identifier plus the parameters needed to project between systems." .

prim:Latitude a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Coordinate ;
    rdfs:label "Latitude" ;
    rdfs:comment "A latitude — the north-south angular Coordinate, ranging from −90 degrees at the south pole to +90 degrees at the north pole, modelled as a subclass of Coordinate. Latitude is one axis of a geographic CRS; its bounded range and its pairing with Longitude are what make a geographic Point. It is realized as a Real-tower angle encoding in degrees (or radians) with the range constraint and the datum from its CRS recorded as facets of the realization." .

prim:Longitude a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Coordinate ;
    rdfs:label "Longitude" ;
    rdfs:comment "A longitude — the east-west angular Coordinate, ranging from −180 to +180 degrees about a prime meridian, modelled as a subclass of Coordinate. Longitude is the other geographic axis paired with Latitude; its wrap-around at the antimeridian is a documented facet any realization must handle. It is realized as a Real-tower angle encoding in degrees (or radians) with the prime meridian and datum from its associated CRS recorded as facets of the realization." .

# ---- Tower 11: Enum / Ordinal -------------------------------------------------------

prim:Enumeration a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Enumeration" ;
    rdfs:comment "An enumeration — a type whose values are drawn from a finite, explicitly named set of members, the root of the enum tower and the parent of Ordinal and Categorical. An Enumeration fixes its members up front; the distinction between the ordered and unordered readings is what its two subtypes capture. It is realized by assigning each member a distinct small integer code and encoding that code; the code-to-member mapping is part of the enumeration's identity, not an arbitrary realization choice." .

prim:Ordinal a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration ;
    rdfs:label "Ordinal" ;
    rdfs:comment "An ordinal enumeration — a finite named set of members that additionally carries a total order, such as low < medium < high or the ordered positions of the 21-column Atlas ring, modelled as a subclass of Enumeration. Ordinal adds a comparison relation the base Enumeration lacks; ranking and monotone operations are meaningful on it. It is realized by assigning order-preserving integer codes so that the numeric ordering of the codes reproduces the intended member ordering exactly." .

prim:Categorical a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration ;
    rdfs:label "Categorical" ;
    rdfs:comment "A categorical enumeration — a finite named set of members with no meaningful order among them, such as colour names or country codes, modelled as a subclass of Enumeration. Categorical is the complement of Ordinal: its integer codes are arbitrary labels and any comparison of them beyond equality is meaningless, so realizations must not leak a spurious order. It is realized by assigning each member a distinct code purely for identity, with equality the only admissible operation on the codes." .

# ---- Tower 12: Binary / Opaque ------------------------------------------------------

prim:Blob a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Blob" ;
    rdfs:comment "A binary large object — opaque byte content with no interpreted internal structure at this level, the hexadecimal or serialized-binary payload that a format leaves uninterpreted. Blob is the root of the binary/opaque tower and the parent of Hash; it is the formal type whose realization is the byte carrier itself with no further formal meaning attached. It is realized directly as a prim:ByteVector or Stream of raw octets; any structure it may have is deferred to a more specific type." .

prim:Hash a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:Blob ;
    rdfs:label "Hash" ;
    rdfs:comment "A hash or digest — a fixed-width opaque byte value produced by a hash function such as sha256, modelled as a subclass of Blob because it is opaque bytes with the extra invariant of a fixed width and a producing algorithm. Hash grounds the atom z-coordinate, which the CRS sub-project derives as the uint16 of the first two octets of a source digest. It is realized as a fixed-length ByteVector (32 octets for sha256) with the algorithm identity recorded as a facet." .

# =====================================================================================
# STEP A — THE FORMAL OLOG AS A GENUINE (THIN) CATEGORY  (W2 · SP1 enrichment)
# =====================================================================================
# The panel residual "X is also grounded through its ancestor" is TRUE categorically but
# was UNMATERIALIZED. This block makes subtyping FIRST-CLASS: the 59 rdfs:subClassOf edges
# (plus every arrow into the FormalType root) become prim:SubtypeArrow individuals with an
# explicit prim:arrowDom and prim:arrowCod; every object carries its prim:IdentityArrow
# prim:id_<T>; and every composable pair along a subtyping chain carries its prim:CompositeArrow
# reifying its canonical decomposition (composeArrowFirst then composeArrowSecond, composeArrowInto
# the composite itself). Because each tower class has exactly ONE direct parent, the Hasse
# diagram is a tree and the induced category is a THIN category (a poset): between any ordered
# pair of objects X <: Y there is AT MOST ONE arrow, so the canonical left-nested decomposition
# of a composite is the ONLY decomposition and Kleisli-style associativity g∘(f) is automatic —
# any two bracketings of the same chain name the same composite arrow. This is the object/arrow
# substrate on which realization.ttl transports ρ over arrows and taiji.ttl closes literal Yoneda.
# These morphism classes are deliberately NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType (they are arrows,
# not objects) so the dual-grounding law (DualGroundingShape / q_universality) does not — and must
# not — demand a physical realization of an arrow; only objects (FormalTypes) are dual-grounded.
# =====================================================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Morphism classes of the formal category (objects are the FormalTypes above)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
prim:FormalArrow a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "FormalArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "A morphism (arrow) of the formal olog considered as a category: a directed edge from one FormalType object (its prim:arrowDom) to another (its prim:arrowCod) witnessing that the domain IS-A subtype of the codomain in the ISA of the mathematics. FormalArrow is the common supertype of the three concrete arrow species prim:SubtypeArrow (a direct rdfs:subClassOf cover edge), prim:IdentityArrow (the reflexive loop id_T at each object) and prim:CompositeArrow (a transitive edge reifying its own decomposition). It is deliberately NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType — an arrow is a morphism between objects, not itself a tower object, so the dual-grounding law never demands a physical realization of it; realization.ttl instead transports the realization functor ρ ACROSS these arrows, and taiji.ttl builds the representable presheaves out of their hom-sets. The formal category they populate is thin (a poset): at most one FormalArrow per ordered pair of objects." .

prim:SubtypeArrow a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:label "SubtypeArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "A generating (cover) morphism of the formal category: the first-class reification of exactly one rdfs:subClassOf edge A ⊂ B, with prim:arrowDom A and prim:arrowCod B. There is one prim:SubtypeArrow per direct subtyping edge among the tower classes — 59 in this floor, one for every immediate-parent relationship including the arrows whose codomain is the FormalType root. SubtypeArrows are the generators of the thin category: every non-identity morphism is either a SubtypeArrow or a composite of SubtypeArrows along a chain, so they carry the whole subtyping order. Each corresponds to a genuine, inspectable rdfs:subClassOf triple in the same file, so the arrow layer never asserts a subtyping relation the class layer does not already hold — the two are kept in exact agreement (Präriehund honesty)." .

prim:IdentityArrow a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:label "IdentityArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "The identity morphism id_T of an object T in the formal category: the reflexive arrow with prim:arrowDom and prim:arrowCod both equal to T, the unit for arrow composition (id_B ∘ f = f = f ∘ id_A for every arrow f: A → B). There is exactly one prim:IdentityArrow per object of the category — 60 in this floor, one for each of the 59 tower classes and one for the FormalType root object itself — so identities are total over the objects, a requirement for the objects+arrows to form a genuine category rather than a bare directed graph. The identity arrow id_T is also the reflexive element of the representable presheaf Hom(-, T): it is the unique member of Hom(T, T) in this thin category, the Yoneda point at which yo(T) evaluated at T contains the identity that the Yoneda lemma pins every natural transformation to." .

prim:CompositeArrow a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:label "CompositeArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "A composite (transitive) morphism of the formal category: the arrow A → C obtained by composing two arrows that meet at a middle object, reifying its own canonical decomposition through prim:composeArrowFirst (the arrow f: A → B applied first), prim:composeArrowSecond (the arrow g: B → C applied second) and prim:composeArrowInto (the resulting composite arrow, this individual itself), with prim:arrowDom A and prim:arrowCod C. One prim:CompositeArrow exists for every pair A <: C where C is a non-immediate ancestor of A — 45 in this floor. The decomposition is canonical and left-nested: composeArrowFirst is always the direct SubtypeArrow from A to its immediate parent P, and composeArrowSecond is the arrow P → C (a direct SubtypeArrow when C covers P, otherwise the higher CompositeArrow P → C). Because the category is THIN (one arrow per ordered pair) this is the unique decomposition, so composition is associative on the nose — (h∘g)∘f and h∘(g∘f) name the very same CompositeArrow — and this individual is that shared witness. This is distinct from the Kleisli prim:Composition of realization.ttl (a reified >=> of realization arrows using prim:composeFirst/Second/Into); a CompositeArrow composes SUBTYPING arrows in the formal olog, which ρ then transports." .

prim:RepresentablePresheaf a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "RepresentablePresheaf" ;
    rdfs:comment "The hook for the Yoneda embedding yo: FormalOlog → PSh(FormalOlog): for each object A the representable presheaf Hom(-, A), materialised here as a prim:RepresentablePresheaf individual prim:repr_<A> that prim:representableOf the object A and whose prim:homSetHasArrow edges enumerate exactly the arrows of the category whose prim:arrowCod is A — i.e. the disjoint hom-sets Hom(X, A) over all objects X. In this thin category each Hom(X, A) is empty or a singleton, so repr_<A>'s materialised hom-set is precisely the down-set of objects X <: A, one arrow apiece (the SubtypeArrow, IdentityArrow or CompositeArrow X → A). This is the object action of the representable presheaf; taiji.ttl (STEP C) lifts it to the full literal Yoneda embedding, makes ρ a natural transformation over it, and — per Directive 2 (ologs of ologs, turtles all the way) — treats each representable as itself an olog object. It is NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at this layer (the Yoneda re-internalisation of representables as tower objects is materialised in taiji.ttl), so the dual-grounding law is not triggered on the presheaf hook." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Properties of the formal category
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
prim:arrowDom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "arrowDom" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The domain (source object) of a FormalArrow: the FormalType the morphism departs from. For a prim:SubtypeArrow reifying A ⊂ B this is A; for prim:IdentityArrow id_T it is T; for a prim:CompositeArrow A → C it is A. Together with prim:arrowCod it types every arrow as a morphism between two genuine tower objects, so the objects+arrows form a category (not a loose graph); realization.ttl reads arrowDom/arrowCod to place each realizationTransport ρ(f) between rho(arrowDom) and rho(arrowCod), and taiji.ttl reads arrowCod to group arrows into the representable hom-sets." .

prim:arrowCod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "arrowCod" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The codomain (target object) of a FormalArrow: the FormalType the morphism lands in. For a prim:SubtypeArrow reifying A ⊂ B this is B; for prim:IdentityArrow id_T it is T; for a prim:CompositeArrow A → C it is C. The codomain is the axis along which representable presheaves are formed: prim:repr_<A> gathers by prim:homSetHasArrow exactly those arrows whose arrowCod is A, materialising Hom(-, A). Codomain agreement (f.arrowCod = g.arrowDom) is also the well-typedness condition a CompositeArrow's composeArrowFirst/Second must satisfy for the composite to exist." .

prim:composeArrowFirst a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeArrowFirst" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:CompositeArrow ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The first operand f of a composite arrow's canonical decomposition h = g ∘ f: the arrow applied FIRST, always the direct prim:SubtypeArrow from the composite's domain A to its immediate parent P. Read together with prim:composeArrowSecond (the arrow g: P → C) and prim:composeArrowInto (the composite h: A → C, this individual), it makes a CompositeArrow a self-describing witness of one composition equation. Well-typedness requires composeArrowFirst.arrowCod = composeArrowSecond.arrowDom (the operands meet at P) and the composite to span them (h.arrowDom = f.arrowDom, h.arrowCod = g.arrowCod). It is deliberately named apart from the Kleisli prim:composeFirst so the formal-olog composition and the realization-monad composition never collide." .

prim:composeArrowSecond a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeArrowSecond" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:CompositeArrow ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The second operand g of a composite arrow's canonical decomposition h = g ∘ f: the arrow applied SECOND, running from the middle object P (the immediate parent of the composite's domain) up to the composite's codomain C. It is a direct prim:SubtypeArrow when C covers P and a higher prim:CompositeArrow P → C otherwise, giving a left-nested canonical form. With prim:composeArrowFirst and prim:composeArrowInto it reifies the composition; because the category is thin this second operand is uniquely determined, which is why associativity holds definitionally — every bracketing of a subtyping chain resolves to the same composite arrow." .

prim:composeArrowInto a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeArrowInto" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:CompositeArrow ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The result h of a composite arrow's decomposition h = g ∘ f: the composite morphism A → C that composeArrowFirst (f) and composeArrowSecond (g) compose to. In this thin category the composite of a given ordered pair is unique, so composeArrowInto points at THIS CompositeArrow individual itself — the arrow is its own composition witness. This mirrors the shape of the Kleisli prim:composeInto in realization.ttl (whose Composition's Into is the composed realization arrow) so that STEP B can transport composition through ρ edge-for-edge: ρ(g ∘ f) = ρ(g) ∘ ρ(f) reads composeArrowFirst/Second/Into here and the parallel Kleisli composeFirst/Second/Into on the transported side." .

prim:representableOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "representableOf" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a prim:RepresentablePresheaf prim:repr_<A> to the object A it represents: repr_<A> IS the representable presheaf Hom(-, A), the image of A under the Yoneda embedding yo. The map A ↦ repr_<A> is injective by construction (one representable per object, distinct IRIs), the object-level witness of Yoneda faithfulness that STEP C's q_yoneda_faithful checks. representableOf pins which object's hom-functor a presheaf is, so ρ's naturality square and the tie of the Frame (the Yoneda point) to Yoneda evaluation can name the representable by the object it stands for." .

prim:homSetHasArrow a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "homSetHasArrow" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "Enumerates the arrows in a representable presheaf's materialised hom-set: prim:repr_<A> prim:homSetHasArrow ?f for exactly those FormalArrows ?f whose prim:arrowCod is A. This is the object action of Hom(-, A): the disjoint union over all objects X of Hom(X, A), which in this thin category is the down-set of X <: A with one arrow (SubtypeArrow, IdentityArrow, or CompositeArrow) per X. The identity arrow id_A is always present (the reflexive element of Hom(A, A)), the Yoneda point the representable pivots on. STEP C reads these hom-sets to build the presheaf's restriction maps and the naturality of ρ; distinct objects yield distinct hom-sets (Yoneda faithfulness)." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SubtypeArrows — one first-class morphism per rdfs:subClassOf cover edge (59)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
prim:sub_Bag_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Bag ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Bag ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Bag ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Bag, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Bignum_Integer a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Bignum ; prim:arrowCod prim:Integer ;
    rdfs:label "Bignum ⊂ Integer" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Bignum ⊂ prim:Integer: prim:arrowDom prim:Bignum, prim:arrowCod prim:Integer. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:Integer)." .
prim:sub_BlankNode_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:BlankNode ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "BlankNode ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:BlankNode ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:BlankNode, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Blob_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Blob ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Blob ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Blob ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Blob, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Bool_Kleene3 a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Bool ; prim:arrowCod prim:Kleene3 ;
    rdfs:label "Bool ⊂ Kleene3" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Bool ⊂ prim:Kleene3: prim:arrowDom prim:Bool, prim:arrowCod prim:Kleene3. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:Kleene3)." .
prim:sub_CRS_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:CRS ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "CRS ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:CRS ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:CRS, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Categorical_Enumeration a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Categorical ; prim:arrowCod prim:Enumeration ;
    rdfs:label "Categorical ⊂ Enumeration" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Categorical ⊂ prim:Enumeration: prim:arrowDom prim:Categorical, prim:arrowCod prim:Enumeration. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:Enumeration)." .
prim:sub_Codepoint_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Codepoint ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Codepoint ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Codepoint ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Codepoint, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Complex_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Complex ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Complex ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Complex ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Complex, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Coordinate_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Coordinate ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Coordinate ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Coordinate ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Coordinate, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Currency_UnitOfMeasure a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Currency ; prim:arrowCod prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    rdfs:label "Currency ⊂ UnitOfMeasure" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Currency ⊂ prim:UnitOfMeasure: prim:arrowDom prim:Currency, prim:arrowCod prim:UnitOfMeasure. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:UnitOfMeasure)." .
prim:sub_Date_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Date ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "Date ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:Date ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:Date, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_DateTime_FormalType a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:DateTime ; prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:label "DateTime ⊂ FormalType" ;
    rdfs:comment "The generating subtype morphism reifying the direct rdfs:subClassOf edge prim:DateTime ⊂ prim:FormalType: prim:arrowDom prim:DateTime, prim:arrowCod prim:FormalType. It corresponds one-to-one to the genuine rdfs:subClassOf triple in this file, so the arrow layer stays in exact agreement with the class layer; it is a generator of the thin formal category and one member of the representable hom-set Hom(-, prim:FormalType)." .
prim:sub_Decimal_Rational a prim:SubtypeArrow ; prim:arrowDom prim:Decimal ; prim:arrowCod prim:Rational ;
